A||N||5||darkmagic||help with starter||||1||
Z||000000||darkmagic||06-09-2001||10:46 PM||||I need some help!<P>I was wondering if anyone else is running into this scenario. I have an 87 4 cylinder auto and my starter is tested and it works fine.<P>The problem is that it has problems starting ONLY when the car has been running and the engine compartment is hot.<P>I have removed my heat shield a while back but only now am I having this problem of starting the car after the engine is warm.<P>Should I re-install a heat shield?<P>I don't have the original heat shield anymore, any one suggest where I can get one?<P>Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Jay||06-09-2001||11:24 PM||||I just replaced my starter on my GT Northstar today.  Same problem, difficulty starting when hot.  That seems to be the sign of the beginning of the end for a starter.  I would put the heat shield back, it's there for a reason and it seems that starters are tempermental when it comes to high heat.  I have to fabricate something because the Northstar didn't come with a heat shield, anything will do, like sheet metal, just to give it a bit more protection from direct heat.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Jay||06-09-2001||11:27 PM||||Oh yeah, welcome to the Forum.  If you are in Toronto you could get a heat shield for a couple of bucks from Race-Tech (The Fiero Shop) at 416-747-5728  They are at Weston Road and Steeles 141 Turbine.  Or just go to a scrap yard.  I was at Standard Auto wreckers in Scarborough and they got 3 1984 Fiero's there almost complete.  I'm sure they got all the parts you'll need.||||reg||1
Z||000003||WKDFIRO||06-10-2001||12:48 PM||||So you are saying that it won't start in the morning?<P>The battery should have enough cold cranking amps to turn it over. Its pretty easy an cheap to have the battery checked. The heat shield for the solenoid is pretty important.||||reg||1
Z||000004||starfighter007@msn.com||06-10-2001||12:56 PM||||you definetly have a starter problem I had the same probelem with my 85 v-6 and it also had a heat shield on the starter but I put a rebuild starter from auto zone on it and in stalled the heat shield and after a week or two it started doing the same thing again any way it took two more starters from them  before the problem was corrected .oh every time I brought it in they checked and it worked fine and had to convince them the problem only happens when it gets hot.so just rember you do have astarter problem incase it shows same problem when you put the ne starter in good luck||||reg||1
Z||000005||phreakboy4||06-10-2001||07:37 PM||||Your gonna  have the same problem over and over unless you replace the heatshield, starter solenoids are greatly affected by heat, so when the get hot they stop working, someone before on here mentiioned that you can use a soup can that will fit nicely over it to disperse the heat.<P><BR>84 2M4 coupe                  Sean V||||reg||1
